In the case, neither relying on anecdotes, nor
instrumenting the game to use the play of
individuals' games as if they were in any way
anything but just more complicated anecdotes, since
those single games are very dependent on playing
style [consider extinctionist versus speed ascender
styles and the effect on "objects" generated"], will
answer the question of how often ordinary games come
up lacking _any_ of some, or some one, of the
important objects.

What will work is statistical analysis, and posters
here have done that kind of analysis many times in
the past.

The range of Dungeon possible levels for each
branch, and probability of each depth, are well
known (where Ludius is considered to have depths
"zero" or "one" for simplicity).

The probabilities for alternative levels for Mines
Town, Mines End, Sokoban, and so on are well known.

The possible shop count distribution for each
variant of Mines Town are well known.

The fixed objects or range of objects that are
"salted" into some special spots in the dungeon
(like Vlad's "oLS, the castle /oW, the Sokoban
prize, shops, etc.) are well known.

The probability of special rooms on ordinary levels
(throne rooms, leprechaun halls, bee hives, shops,
temples, etc) is well known.

The behavior of the rgrn in populating various sorts
of dungeon branch levels is well known, including
Mines Town and treasure zoos.

For monster initial equipment and death drops, some
agreed upon number of monsters (around 2500 seems to
be a common "kill count" in many ascended games) can
be used, the fraction of them that can do death
drops can be analyzed by their creation
probabilities going down and going back up on a "PC
is at least XL 14, and per DLVL" basis for monster
generation, minus also the special monsters that
can't have death drops (demon princes, maybe?).

Based on all that, the initial distribution of
objects generated is calculatable exactly for all
but monster initial equipment and death drops, and
the probability of zero of each interesting sort,
and the cumulative probability of zero of at least
one interesting sort of object, may be calculated or
learned via simulations with repeated runs.

Any of the people that enjoy doing this stuff
willing to bite off this big a task?

